const t={"karma-theory":{id:"karma-theory",category:"tattva",title:"Karma Theory",subtitle:"Law of Cause and Effect in Jainism",type:"html",content:`<div class="book-content">\r
    <p style="text-align: center; margin-bottom: 2rem; font-size: 1.1rem; line-height: 1.6;">\r
        In Jainism, <strong>Karma</strong> is not just a metaphysical concept but a real substance (matter)\r
        that gets attached to the soul.\r
        It is the primary cause of the soul's transmigration in the cycle of birth and death (Samsara).\r
        Karmas are classified into 8 main types, split into two categories: <strong>Ghatiya</strong>\r
        (Destructive) and <strong>Aghatiya</strong> (Non-Destructive).\r
    </p>\r
\r
    <!-- Ghatiya Karmas -->\r
    <div class="karma-section ghatiya">\r
        <h2 class="karma-type-title">\r
            <span style="color: #ef4444;">🚫</span>\r
            Ghatiya Karmas (Destructive)\r
        </h2>\r
        <p>These karmas directly destroy the natural qualities of the soul (Knowledge, Perception, Bliss,\r
            Power).</p>\r
\r
        <div class="karma-grid">\r
            <div class="karma-card">\r
                <div class="karma-name">1. Jnanavaraniya</div>\r
                <div class="karma-desc"><strong>Knowledge Obscuring:</strong> Obstructs the soul's infinite\r
                    knowledge (Kevala Jnana). Like a blindfold covering the eyes.</div>\r
            </div>\r
            <div class="karma-card">\r
                <div class="karma-name">2. Darshanavaraniya</div>\r
                <div class="karma-desc"><strong>Perception Obscuring:</strong> Obstructs the soul's infinite\r
                    perception (Darshan). Like a guard preventing audience with the King.</div>\r
            </div>\r
            <div class="karma-card">\r
                <div class="karma-name">3. Mohaniya</div>\r
                <div class="karma-desc"><strong>Deluding:</strong> The most dangerous karma. It causes\r
                    delusion about the self and creates passions (anger, pride, etc.). Like intoxication.\r
                </div>\r
            </div>\r
            <div class="karma-card">\r
                <div class="karma-name">4. Antaraya</div>\r
                <div class="karma-desc"><strong>Obstructing:</strong> Hinders specific capabilities like\r
                    charity, gain, and willpower. Like a treasurer refusing to release funds.</div>\r
            </div>\r
        </div>\r
    </div>\r
\r
    <!-- Aghatiya Karmas -->\r
    <div class="karma-section aghatiya">\r
        <h2 class="karma-type-title">\r
            <span style="color: #22c55e;">🍃</span>\r
            Aghatiya Karmas (Non-Destructive)\r
        </h2>\r
        <p>These karmas do not harm the soul's nature but determine the external conditions of existence\r
            (Body, Status, Age).</p>\r
\r
        <div class="karma-grid">\r
            <div class="karma-card">\r
                <div class="karma-name">5. Vedaniya</div>\r
                <div class="karma-desc"><strong>Feeling Producing:</strong> Causes feelings of pleasure\r
                    (Shata) or pain (Ashata). Like licking honey from a sword blade.</div>\r
            </div>\r
            <div class="karma-card">\r
                <div class="karma-name">6. Ayu</div>\r
                <div class="karma-desc"><strong>Age Determining:</strong> Determines the lifespan in a\r
                    particular realm (Hell, Heaven, Human, Animal). Like chains binding a prisoner.</div>\r
            </div>\r
            <div class="karma-card">\r
                <div class="karma-name">7. Nama</div>\r
                <div class="karma-desc"><strong>Physique Determining:</strong> Determines the body type,\r
                    beauty, strength, and voice. Like a painter drawing a picture.</div>\r
            </div>\r
            <div class="karma-card">\r
                <div class="karma-name">8. Gotra</div>\r
                <div class="karma-desc"><strong>Status Determining:</strong> Determines high or low social\r
                    status and environment. Like a potter making high or low quality pots.</div>\r
            </div>\r
        </div>\r
    </div>\r
</div>`},"jiva-tattva":{id:"jiva-tattva",category:"tattva",title:"Jiva (Soul)",subtitle:"The First Tattva - The Living Entity",type:"html",content:`<div class="book-content">
    <h3>🌟 What is Jiva?</h3>
    <p><strong>Jiva</strong> (soul) is a living, conscious entity possessing infinite knowledge, infinite perception, infinite bliss, and infinite power in its pure state. Unlike lifeless matter (Ajiva), Jiva has consciousness (Chetana) and the ability to experience happiness and sorrow.</p>

    <div class="concept-card">
        <h3>Characteristics of Jiva</h3>
        <ul class="vows-list">
            <li><strong>Consciousness (Chetana):</strong> The defining characteristic that separates soul from matter.</li>
            <li><strong>Formless (Amurta):</strong> Soul has no physical form, color, or smell.</li>
            <li><strong>Equal to Body Size:</strong> The soul expands or contracts to fill the body it inhabits (like lamplight in a room).</li>
            <li><strong>Eternal (Nitya):</strong> Soul is never created nor destroyed - only transforms.</li>
            <li><strong>Infinite Capabilities:</strong> Each soul possesses infinite knowledge (Anant Jnana), infinite perception (Anant Darshan), infinite bliss (Anant Sukha), and infinite power (Anant Virya) when liberated.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="concept-card">
        <h3>Classification of Jivas</h3>
        <p><strong>Based on Senses:</strong></p>
        <ul class="vows-list">
            <li><strong>Ekendriya:</strong> One-sensed (touch only) - Plants, microorganisms</li>
            <li><strong>Dvindriya:</strong> Two-sensed (touch + taste) - Worms, leeches</li>
            <li><strong>Trindriya:</strong> Three-sensed (+ smell) - Ants, lice</li>
            <li><strong>Chaturindriya:</strong> Four-sensed (+ sight) - Bees, mosquitoes</li>
            <li><strong>Panchendriya:</strong> Five-sensed (+ hearing) - Animals, birds, humans, gods, hell-beings</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="margin-top: 1rem;"><strong>Based on Liberation State:</strong></p>
        <ul class="vows-list">
            <li><strong>Samsari Jiva:</strong> Souls wandering in the cycle of birth and death</li>
            <li><strong>Mukta Jiva (Siddha):</strong> Liberated souls who have destroyed all karmas</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="quote-box">
        "The soul is your true self. Realizing this is the first step toward liberation."
    </div>
</div>`},"ajiva-tattva":{id:"ajiva-tattva",category:"tattva",title:"Ajiva (Non-Soul)",subtitle:"The Second Tattva - Lifeless Entities",type:"html",content:`<div class="book-content">
    <h3>💎 What is Ajiva?</h3>
    <p><strong>Ajiva</strong> refers to all lifeless entities that lack consciousness. While Jiva is the only conscious entity, Ajiva comprises everything else in the universe. Ajiva does not experience pleasure or pain and cannot attain liberation.</p>

    <div class="concept-card">
        <h3>Five Types of Ajiva (Pancha Dravya)</h3>
        <ul class="vows-list">
            <li><strong>1. Pudgala (Matter):</strong> Has form, color, taste, smell, and touch. Includes all physical objects, karma particles, and our bodies. Can be perceived by senses.</li>
            <li><strong>2. Dharma Dravya (Medium of Motion):</strong> Invisible substance that enables souls and matter to move. Like water helps fish swim, Dharma helps movement. Not "religion" but a physical medium.</li>
            <li><strong>3. Adharma Dravya (Medium of Rest):</strong> Invisible substance that enables souls and matter to rest/stop. Like shade providing rest to travelers.</li>
            <li><strong>4. Akasha (Space):</strong> Provides room for all substances to exist. Infinite and formless. Without space, nothing could exist.</li>
            <li><strong>5. Kala (Time):</strong> Enables change, transformation, and  movement. Helps measure duration and sequence of events.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="concept-card">
        <h3>Key Differences: Jiva vs Ajiva</h3>
        <table style="width: 100%; margin-top: 1rem;">
            <tr>
                <th style="text-align: left; padding: 0.5rem;">Jiva</th>
                <th style="text-align: left; padding: 0.5rem;">Ajiva</th>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="padding: 0.5rem;">Conscious (Chetana)</td>
                <td style="padding: 0.5rem;">Unconscious (Acetana)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="padding: 0.5rem;">Can attain liberation</td>
                <td style="padding: 0.5rem;">Cannot attain liberation</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="padding: 0.5rem;">Experiences karma</td>
                <td style="padding: 0.5rem;">Does not experience karma</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="padding: 0.5rem;">Formless (Amurta)</td>
                <td style="padding: 0.5rem;">Matter has form, others formless</td>
            </tr>
        </table>
    </div>

    <div class="quote-box">
        "Understanding that the body, wealth, and worldly objects are Ajiva (non-self) helps develop detachment."
    </div>
</div>`},"asrava-tattva":{id:"asrava-tattva",category:"tattva",title:"Asrava (Influx of Karma)",subtitle:"The Third Tattva - How Karma Enters the Soul",type:"html",content:`<div class="book-content">
    <h3>🌊 What is Asrava?</h3>
    <p><strong>Asrava</strong> literally means "influx" or "inflow." It is the process through which karmic particles flow into and attach to the soul, like dust sticking to a wet cloth. Asrava is caused by three doors: wrong belief, lack of self-control, and passionate activity.</p>

    <div class="concept-card">
        <h3>The 42 Channels of Asrava</h3>
        <p>Karma enters the soul through 42 channels (doors), classified into major categories:</p>
        
        <p style="font-weight: 600; margin-top: 1rem;">1. Five Senses (5):</p>
        <ul class="vows-list">
            <li>Touch, Taste, Smell, Sight, Hearing - Using senses with attachment/aversion</li>
        </ul>

        <p style="font-weight: 600; margin-top: 1rem;">2. Four Passions (Kashaya) - 16 types:</p>
        <ul class="vows-list">
            <li><strong>Anger (Krodha):</strong> Intense, moderate, mild, subtle (4 types)</li>
            <li><strong>Pride (Mana):</strong> Intense, moderate, mild, subtle (4 types)</li>
            <li><strong>Deceit (Maya):</strong> Intense, moderate, mild, subtle (4 types)</li>
            <li><strong>Greed (Lobha):</strong> Intense, moderate, mild, subtle (4 types)</li>
        </ul>

        <p style="font-weight: 600; margin-top: 1rem;">3. Five Avirati (Non-Restraint) - 5:</p>
        <ul class="vows-list">
            <li>Violence, lying, stealing, sensuality, attachment to possessions</li>
        </ul>

        <p style="font-weight: 600; margin-top: 1rem;">4. Activities (Yoga) - 15:</p>
        <ul class="vows-list">
            <li>Mental (4), Verbal (4), and Physical (7) activities done with karmic intent</li>
        </ul>

        <p style="font-weight: 600; margin-top: 1rem;">5. Wrong Belief (Mithyatva) - 1:</p>
        <ul class="vows-list">
            <li>Not believing in the soul, karma, liberation, or true teachers</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="concept-card">
        <h3>How Does Karma Stick?</h3>
        <p>Karma particles are everywhere, but they only stick to the soul when there are <strong>passions (Kashaya)</strong>. Think of it like:</p>
        <ul class="vows-list">
            <li><strong>Dry Cloth:</strong> Dust won't stick easily (passionless soul)</li>
            <li><strong>Wet Cloth:</strong> Dust sticks readily (soul with passions)</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">The stronger the passion, the longer and more intense the karma bondage. This is why controlling anger, pride, deceit, and greed is essential.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="quote-box">
        "Every thought, word, and deed done with passion is a door for karma to enter."
    </div>
</div>`},"bandha-tattva":{id:"bandha-tattva",category:"tattva",title:"Bandha (Bondage of Karma)",subtitle:"The Fourth Tattva - The Binding of Karma to Soul",type:"html",content:`<div class="book-content">
    <h3>⛓️ What is Bandha?</h3>
    <p><strong>Bandha</strong> means "bondage" - the actual attachment of karmic particles to the soul. While Asrava is the influx of karma, Bandha is when those particles actually bind to the soul and determine its future experiences. The nature of bondage depends on the intensity of passions at the time of action.</p>

    <div class="concept-card">
        <h3>Four Aspects of Bandha</h3>
        <ul class="vows-list">
            <li><strong>1. Prakriti Bandha (Nature):</strong> What TYPE of karma binds - one of the 8 main karmas or 148 sub-types. Determined by the nature of your action (e.g., violence binds Ayu karma for lower birth).</li>
            <li><strong>2. Sthiti Bandha (Duration):</strong> How LONG the karma will stay attached before giving fruit. Can be from a moment to many lifetimes. Depends on intensity of passion.</li>
            <li><strong>3. Anubhaga Bandha (Intensity):</strong> How STRONG the fruition will be when karma matures. Like mild anger binds mild-intensity karma, fierce anger binds intense karma.</li>
            <li><strong>4. Pradesh Bandha (Quantity):</strong> How MANY karma particles bind. More passion = more particles.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="concept-card">
        <h3>Example of How Karma Binds</h3>
        <p><strong>Scenario:</strong> A person angrily strikes someone.</p>
        <ul class="vows-list">
            <li><strong>Nature:</strong> Violent action binds karma that will result in painful experiences (Vedaniya) and lower birth (Ayu).</li>
            <li><strong>Duration:</strong> Intense anger makes karma stay attached for many lifetimes.</li>
            <li><strong>Intensity:</strong> The greater the anger, the more severe the future suffering.</li>
            <li><strong>Quantity:</strong> Immense anger binds countless karma particles.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="margin-top: 1rem;"><strong>Key Point:</strong> If the same violent act is done without anger (e.g., a doctor performing surgery), minimal karma binds because passion is absent!</p>
    </div>

    <div class="concept-card">
        <h3>The Role of Kashaya (Passions)</h3>
        <p>Passions are like <strong>glue</strong> - they determine how karma sticks:</p>
        <ul class="vows-list">
            <li><strong>Intense Passion:</strong> Karma binds very strongly, for long duration</li>
            <li><strong>Mild Passion:</strong> Weaker bondage, shorter duration</li>
            <li><strong>No Passion:</strong> Minimal or no bondage (actions of enlightened beings)</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="quote-box">
        "It's not the action alone, but the passion behind it that determines karma bondage."
    </div>
</div>`},"samvara-tattva":{id:"samvara-tattva",category:"tattva",title:"Samvara (Stoppage of Karma)",subtitle:"The Fifth Tattva - Preventing New Karma",type:"html",content:`<div class="book-content">
    <h3>🛡️ What is Samvara?</h3>
    <p><strong>Samvara</strong> means "stoppage" or "blocking." It is the process of closing the channels (Asrava) through which new karma enters the soul. Like closing the doors to prevent rain from entering a house, Samvara prevents new karmic bondage. This is achieved through right conduct, self-control, and spiritual discipline.</p>

    <div class="concept-card">
        <h3>57 Methods of Samvara</h3>
        <p>Jain scriptures describe 57 ways to stop karmic influx. These are grouped into major categories:</p>
        
        <p style="font-weight: 600; margin-top: 1rem;">1. Gupti (3) - Three Restraints:</p>
        <ul class="vows-list">
            <li><strong>Mano-Gupti:</strong> Control of mind - preventing evil thoughts</li>
            <li><strong>Vachana-Gupti:</strong> Control of speech - speaking truth, avoiding harsh words</li>
            <li><strong>Kaya-Gupti:</strong> Control of body - avoiding violent actions</li>
        </ul>

        <p style="font-weight: 600; margin-top: 1rem;">2. Samiti (5) - Five Carefulness:</p>
        <ul class="vows-list">
            <li><strong>Irya Samiti:</strong> Carefulness in walking (to avoid harming small creatures)</li>
            <li><strong>Bhasha Samiti:</strong> Carefulness in speaking (measured, truthful speech)</li>
            <li><strong>Eshana Samiti:</strong> Carefulness in accepting food (ensuring purity)</li>
            <li><strong>Adana-Nikshepana Samiti:</strong> Carefulness in picking/placing objects</li>
            <li><strong>Utsarga Samiti:</strong> Carefulness in excretion (proper waste disposal)</li>
        </ul>

        <p style="font-weight: 600; margin-top: 1rem;">3. Dharma (10) - Ten Virtues:</p>
        <ul class="vows-list">
            <li>Forgiveness, Humility, Straightforwardness, Truth, Purity, Self-Control, Penance, Renunciation, Non-Attachment, Celibacy</li>
        </ul>

        <p style="font-weight: 600; margin-top: 1rem;">4. Bhavana (12) - Twelve Contemplations:</p>
        <ul class="vows-list">
            <li>Impermanence, Helplessness, Samsara's cycle, Loneliness, etc.</li>
        </ul>

        <p style="font-weight: 600; margin-top: 1rem;">5. Parishaha Jaya (22) - Conquering Hardships:</php>
        <ul class="vows-list">
            <li>Enduring hunger, thirst, cold, heat, insect bites, etc., without anger</li>
        </ul>

        <p style="font-weight: 600; margin-top: 1rem;">6. Charitra (5) - Five Types of Conduct:</p>
        <ul class="vows-list">
            <li>Samyak Darshan (Right Belief), Vrata (Vows), Avirati (Partial Restraint), etc.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="quote-box">
        "To empty a pond, first stop the inlet. Similarly, to free the soul, first stop new karma through Samvara."
    </div>
</div>`},"nirjara-tattva":{id:"nirjara-tattva",category:"tattva",title:"Nirjara (Shedding of Karma)",subtitle:"The Sixth Tattva - Removing Existing Karma",type:"html",content:`<div class="book-content">
    <h3>🔥 What is Nirjara?</h3>
    <p><strong>Nirjara</strong> means "falling off" or "shedding" - the process of removing accumulated karma from the soul. While Samvara stops new karma from entering, Nirjara removes the existing stockpile. Like repaying a debt, Nirjara exhausts karmic bondage through experience (Bhava Nirjara) or tapas/austerities (Dravya Nirjara).</p>

    <div class="concept-card">
        <h3>Two Types of Nirjara</h3>
        
        <div style="margin: 1rem 0; padding: 1rem; background: #fff5f2; border-left: 4px solid var(--primary-saffron); border-radius: 8px;">
            <h4 style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0;">1. Savipaka Nirjara (With Fruition)</h4>
            <p style="margin: 0;">Karma naturally gives its fruit when it matures and then falls off. This is automatic - you experience the result of past karma (pleasure/pain) and it is exhausted. This happens to everyone, even without spiritual effort.</p>
            <p style="margin-top: 0.5rem; font-style: italic;">Example: Suffering an illness due to past violent karma - once experienced, that specific karma is shed.</p>
        </div>

        <div style="margin: 1rem 0; padding: 1rem; background: #f0fdf4; border-left: 4px solid var(--kumkum-red); border-radius: 8px;">
            <h4 style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0;">2. Avipaka Nirjara (Without Fruition)</h4>
            <p style="margin: 0;">Karma is forcefully shed through <strong>austerities (Tapa)</strong> BEFORE it gives fruit - like burning seeds before they sprout. This is voluntary and requires spiritual effort. Only this type leads to liberation.</p>
            <p style="margin-top: 0.5rem; font-style: italic;">Example: Through intense meditation and fasting, a monk destroys karma that was scheduled to give fruit in future lives.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="concept-card">
        <h3>12 Types of Tapas (Austerities) for Nirjara</h3>
        
        <p style="font-weight: 600;">External Tapas (Bahya Tapa) - 6:</p>
        <ul class="vows-list">
            <li><strong>1. Anashana:</strong> Complete fasting</li>
            <li><strong>2. Unodari:</strong> Eating less than one's fill</li>
            <li><strong>3. Vritti Parisankhyana:</strong> Taking food from limited sources</li>
            <li><strong>4. Rasa Parityaga:</strong> Giving up tasty foods</li>
            <li><strong>5. Vivikta Shayyasana:</strong> Staying in isolated places</li>
            <li><strong>6. Kaya Klesha:</strong> Enduring physical hardships (standing meditation, etc.)</li>
        </ul>

        <p style="font-weight: 600; margin-top: 1rem;">Internal Tapas (Abhyantara Tapa) - 6:</p>
        <ul class="vows-list">
            <li><strong>1. Prayashchitta:</strong> Repentance for wrong actions</li>
            <li><strong>2. Vinaya:</strong> Humility and respect</li>
            <li><strong>3. Vaiyavritya:</strong> Service to monks and elders</li>
            <li><strong>4. Svadhyaya:</strong> Study of scriptures</li>
            <li><strong>5. Vyutsarga:</strong> Meditation and renunciation of body attachment</li>
            <li><strong>6. Dhyana:</strong> Deep meditation on the soul</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="quote-box">
        "Samvara is the dam that stops water; Nirjara is draining the reservoir. Both are needed for liberation."
    </div>
</div>`},"moksha-tattva":{id:"moksha-tattva",category:"tattva",title:"Moksha (Liberation)",subtitle:"The Seventh Tattva - The Ultimate Goal",type:"html",content:`<div class="book-content">
    <h3>🕊️ What is Moksha?</h3>
    <p><strong>Moksha</strong> or <strong>Nirvana</strong> is the state of complete liberation - the soul's eternal freedom from all karma and the cycle of birth and death. It is the ultimate goal of every soul. In liberation, the soul regains its infinite knowledge, infinite perception, infinite bliss, and infinite power. A liberated soul is called a <strong>Siddha</strong>.</p>

    <div class="concept-card">
        <h3>Characteristics of Moksha</h3>
        <ul class="vows-list">
            <li><strong>Total Freedom:</strong> Complete liberation from all 8 types of karma</li>
            <li><strong>Anant Chatustaya:</strong> Four infinitudes restored - Infinite Knowledge (Anant Jnana), Infinite Perception (Anant Darshan), Infinite Bliss (Anant Sukha), Infinite Power (Anant Virya)</li>
            <li><strong>No Rebirth:</strong> End of the cycle of transmigration (Samsara)</li>
            <li><strong>Formlessness:</strong> No body - pure consciousness only</li>
            <li><strong>Eternal:</strong> Once achieved, can never be lost</li>
            <li><strong>Upward Movement:</strong> Liberated souls rise to Siddhashila at the peak of the universe</li>
            <li><strong>Perfect Equanimity:</strong> No attachmentor aversion, complete peace</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="concept-card">
        <h3>The Path to Moksha - Ratnatraya (Three Jewels)</h3>
        <p>Liberation is achieved through the three jewels of Jainism:</p>
        
        <div style="margin: 1rem 0; padding: 1rem; background: #fef3c7; border-left: 4px solid #f59e0b; border-radius: 8px;">
            <h4 style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0;">💎 1. Samyak Darshan (Right Belief)</h4>
            <p style="margin: 0;">Correct understanding and faith in the nine tattvas, seven realities, and teachings of Tirthankaras. Believing that the soul is separate from body and karma.</p>
        </div>

        <div style="margin: 1rem 0; padding: 1rem; background: #fef3c7; border-left: 4px solid #f59e0b; border-radius: 8px;">
            <h4 style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0;">💎 2. Samyak Jnana (Right Knowledge)</h4>
            <p style="margin: 0;">Thorough knowledge of reality as it is - understanding the tattvas, soul-nature, karma theory, and the path to liberation.</p>
        </div>

        <div style="margin: 1rem 0; padding: 1rem; background: #fef3c7; border-left: 4px solid #f59e0b; border-radius: 8px;">
            <h4 style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0;">💎 3. Samyak Charitra (Right Conduct)</h4>
            <p style="margin: 0;">Living according to the five vows (Ahimsa, Satya, Achaurya, Brahmacharya, Aparigraha), practicing austerities, and shedding passions.</p>
        </div>

        <p style="margin-top: 1rem; font-weight: 600;">These three must work together - like a lamp needs container, wick, and oil to burn.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="concept-card">
        <h3>Where Do Liberated Souls Go?</h3>
        <p>After liberation, the Siddha soul rises to <strong>Siddhashila</strong> (Ishatpragbhara Prithvi) - the topmost realm of the universe, located at the very peak of Lokakasha (cosmic space). There, liberated souls exist in eternal bliss, free from all suffering, possessing infinite knowledge of all three times (past, present, future).</p>
        <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">All Siddhas are identical in qualities - all have infinite knowledge, infinite bliss, and infinite power. They neither eat, sleep, nor interact - they exist in pure, perfect consciousness.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="quote-box">
        "The soul's journey: From Samsara (bondage) through Samvara & Nirjara (purification) to Moksha (liberation)."
    </div>
</div>`},"six-dravyas":{id:"six-dravyas",category:"tattva",title:"Shad Dravya (Six Substances)",subtitle:"The Building Blocks of Reality",type:"html",content:`<div class="book-content">
    <h3>🔮 What are Dravyas?</h3>
    <p><strong>Dravya</strong> means 'substance' - the fundamental building blocks of reality. Everything that exists in the universe is made of one or more of these six eternal substances. Understanding the Shad Dravya is essential to Jain metaphysics.</p>

    <div class="concept-card">
        <h3>The Six Dravyas</h3>
        <ul class="vows-list">
            <li><strong>1. Jiva:</strong> Soul - The only conscious substance</li>
            <li><strong>2. Pudgala:</strong> Matter - Has form, perceivable by senses</li>
            <li><strong>3. Dharma:</strong> Medium of Motion - Enables movement</li>
            <li><strong>4. Adharma:</strong> Medium of Rest - Enables stopping</li>
            <li><strong>5. Akasha:</strong> Space - Provides accommodation</li>
            <li><strong>6. Kala:</strong> Time - Enables change</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="margin-top: 1rem;"><strong>Note:</strong> Dharma and Adharma here refer to physical mediums, not religion or morality</p>
    </div>

    <div class="quote-box">
        "The universe consists of these six eternal, indestructible substances interacting together."
    </div>
</div>`},"twelve-vratas":{id:"twelve-vratas",category:"tattva",title:"Barah Vrat (12 Vows for Householders)",subtitle:"The Path of Righteous Living",type:"html",content:`<div class="book-content">
    <h3>🙏 The Lay Person's Code</h3>
    <p>The <strong>12 Vratas</strong> are ethical vows for Jain householders (Shravakas). These partial vows allow spiritual progress while fulfilling worldly duties.</p>

    <div class="concept-card">
        <h3>📿 1-5: Anuvratas (Small Vows)</h3>
        <ul class="vows-list">
            <li><strong>1. Ahimsa:</strong> Non-violence towards mobile beings</li>
            <li><strong>2. Satya:</strong> Truthfulness, avoiding harmful lies</li>
            <li><strong>3. Achaurya:</strong> Not stealing, honest business</li>
            <li><strong>4. Brahmacharya:</strong> Marital fidelity, sensual control</li>
            <li><strong>5. Aparigraha:</strong> Limiting possessions and wealth</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="concept-card">
        <h3>🛡️ 6-8: Gunavratas (Merit Vows)</h3>
        <ul class="vows-list">
            <li><strong>6. Digvrata:</strong> Limiting area of movement</li>
            <li><strong>7. Bhogopabhoga:</strong> Limiting consumables and possessions</li>
            <li><strong>8. Anartha-danda:</strong> Avoiding purposeless sins (gambling, idle talk, etc.)</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="concept-card">
        <h3>🌟 9-12: Shiksha Vratas (Disciplinary Vows)</h3>
        <ul class="vows-list">
            <li><strong>9. Samayika:</strong> Daily meditation (48 min equanimity practice)</li>
            <li><strong>10. Desavakasika:</strong> Further limiting movement for a period</li>
            <li><strong>11. Paushadha:</strong> Living like a monk for 24 hours periodically</li>
            <li><strong>12. Atithi Samvibhaga:</strong> Offering pure food to monks and Jains</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="quote-box">
        "The 12 Vratas bridge worldly life and spiritual liberation for householders."
    </div>
</div>`},anekantavada:{id:"anekantavada",category:"tattva",title:"Anekantavada",subtitle:"The Doctrine of Many-Sidedness",type:"html",content:`<div class="book-content">\r
    <p style="text-align: center; margin-bottom: 2rem; font-size: 1.1rem; line-height: 1.6;">\r
        <strong>Anekantavada</strong> (Non-Absolutism) is the foundation of Jain philosophy. It states that\r
        truth and reality are\r
        complex and have infinite aspects. No single perspective can capture the absolute truth.\r
        It teaches tolerance and open-mindedness by respecting others' viewpoints.\r
    </p>\r
\r
    <div class="concept-card">\r
        <h2 class="concept-title">🐘 The Blind Men and the Elephant</h2>\r
        <p>\r
            The famous parable illustrates Anekantavada perfectly. Several blind men touch different parts\r
            of an elephant:\r
        </p>\r
        <ul class="syadvada-list">\r
            <li>One touches the trunk: "It is like a snake."</li>\r
            <li>One touches the ear: "It is like a fan."</li>\r
            <li>One touches the leg: "It is like a pillar."</li>\r
            <li>One touches the wall: "It is like a wall."</li>\r
        </ul>\r
        <div class="quote-box">\r
            "They are all correct from their limited perspective, but none has seen the whole truth.\r
            The whole elephant is the sum of all parts."\r
        </div>\r
    </div>\r
\r
    <div class="concept-card">\r
        <h2 class="concept-title">👁️ Syadvada (Theory of Relativity)</h2>\r
        <p>\r
            <strong>Syadvada</strong> is the linguistic expression of Anekantavada. It uses the prefix\r
            <strong>"Syat"</strong>\r
            (meaning "in some way" or "from a certain perspective") to make a statement valid.\r
            It prevents dogmatism by acknowledging that a statement is true only under specific conditions.\r
        </p>\r
        <p style="font-weight: 500; margin-top: 1rem;">The Seven-fold Predication (Saptabhangi):</p>\r
        <ul class="syadvada-list">\r
            <li><strong>Syad-asti:</strong> In some ways, it exists.</li>\r
            <li><strong>Syad-nasti:</strong> In some ways, it does not exist.</li>\r
            <li><strong>Syad-asti-nasti:</strong> In some ways, it exists and does not exist.</li>\r
            <li><strong>Syad-avaktavyam:</strong> In some ways, it is indescribable.</li>\r
        </ul>\r
    </div>\r
\r
    <div class="concept-card">\r
        <h2 class="concept-title">🧠 Nayavada (Viewpoints)</h2>\r
        <p>\r
            <strong>Nayavada</strong> is the system of partial viewpoints. Since we are not omniscient, we\r
            can only perceive\r
            reality through specific angles (Nayas).\r
        </p>\r
        <p>\r
            For example, a gold ring is "Gold" from the Substantial Viewpoint (Dravyarthika Naya) relative\r
            to its material,\r
            but it is a "Ring" from the Modal Viewpoint (Paryayarthika Naya) relative to its shape. Both are\r
            true.\r
        </p>\r
    </div>\r
</div>`},gunasthan:{id:"gunasthan",category:"tattva",title:"Gunasthan (Stages of Spiritual Growth)",subtitle:"The 14 Steps to Liberation",type:"html",content:`<div class="book-content"><p><strong>Gunasthan</strong> means "Stages of Virtue." It describes the soul's journey from complete delusion (Mithyatva) to complete liberation (Siddha). As the soul sheds Karma and gains self-realization, it ascends these 14 steps.</p><div class="concept-card"><h3>Steps 1-4: Belief</h3><ul><li><strong>1. Mithyatva:</strong> False Belief. The soul mistakes the body for the self.</li><li><strong>2. Sasadhana:</strong> Falling back from right belief (temporary stage).</li><li><strong>3. Mishra:</strong> Mixed belief (doubtful).</li><li><strong>4. Avirati Samyak Drishti:</strong> Right Belief is attained, but no vows yet. The soul knows the truth but lacks self-control.</li></ul></div><div class="concept-card"><h3>Steps 5-6: Partial Conduct</h3><ul><li><strong>5. Desha Virata:</strong> Partial Vows. The householder stage (Shravak) observing 12 Vratas.</li><li><strong>6. Pramatta Samyata:</strong> Full Vows (Monkhood) but with slight negligence/carelessness.</li></ul></div><div class="concept-card"><h3>Steps 7-12: Purity & Passionlessness</h3><ul><li><strong>7. Apramatta Samyata:</strong> Perfect observance of vows without negligence.</li><li><strong>8-10. Shreni (Ladder):</strong> Rapid destruction of passions (anger, pride, deceit, greed).</li><li><strong>11. Upashanta Moha:</strong> Suppressed passions (can fall back).</li><li><strong>12. Kshina Moha:</strong> Destroyed passions completely. Victory over attachment!</li></ul></div><div class="concept-card"><h3>Steps 13-14: Omniscience</h3><ul><li><strong>13. Sayoga Kevali:</strong> Omniscience with body (Arihant). Teaching Dharma.</li><li><strong>14. Ayoga Kevali:</strong> Omniscience without activity. Just before leaving the body to become a Siddha.</li></ul></div></div>`},leshya:{id:"leshya",category:"tattva",title:"Leshya (Mental Color)",subtitle:"The 6 Colors of Thoughts",type:"html",content:`<div class="book-content"><p><strong>Leshya</strong> refers to the "stain" or color of the soul caused by our thoughts and emotions. It reflects our state of mind and intensity of passions.</p><div class="concept-card"><h3>The Parable of the Tree</h3><p>Six friends see a fruit tree. Their different approaches illustrate the 6 Leshyas:</p><ul><li><strong>1. Krishna (Black):</strong> "Let's uproot the whole tree!" (Maximum violence/cruelty)</li><li><strong>2. Neel (Blue):</strong> "Let's cut the trunk." (Lazy/Deceitful)</li><li><strong>3. Kapot (Grey):</strong> "Let's cut the big branches." (Anger/Envy)</li></ul><p><em>(These three are Inauspicious Leshyas leading to bad karma.)</em></p><ul><li><strong>4. Tejo (Red):</strong> "Let's just pick the bunches." (Prudence/Discipline)</li><li><strong>5. Padma (Yellow):</strong> "Let's pick only ripe fruits." (Compassion/Kindness)</li><li><strong>6. Shukla (White):</strong> "Let's eat the fruits fallen on the ground." (Purest/No harm)</li></ul><p><strong>Lesson:</strong> Always check your thoughts. Are you untying the knot (White) or cutting the whole rope (Black)?</p></div></div>`}};export{t as PhilosophyData};
